  "raw_output": "{\n  \"exercise_type\": \"CEFR English Placement Test\",\n  \"section_id\": 5,\n  \"section_title\": \"Reading Comprehension\",\n  \"cefr_range\": \"B2-C1\",\n  \"reading_text\": \"The concept of 'slow travel' has been gaining popularity in recent years. It involves taking the time to really experience a place, rather than rushing from one tourist destination to another. This approach allows travelers to get a deeper understanding of local cultures and ways of life. For example, instead of visiting a famous museum or landmark, slow travelers might choose to take a cooking class with a local family or attend a traditional festival. By doing so, they can gain a more authentic and meaningful experience of the place they are visiting.\n\nSlow travel is not just about taking it easy, but also about being mindful of one's impact on the environment. Many slow travelers opt for eco-friendly accommodations and transportation options, such as staying in locally-owned guesthouses or using public transport. This approach not only reduces their carbon footprint but also supports local economies.\n\nHowever, some critics argue that slow travel can be elitist, as it often requires a certain level of financial resources. They claim that this approach is inaccessible to many people who cannot afford the luxury of taking their time and indulging in expensive activities. In response, proponents of slow travel point out that there are ways to experience the benefits of slow travel on a budget. For instance, one can stay in hostels or participate in community-based tourism initiatives.\n\nUltimately, the concept of slow travel is not just about how we travel, but also about what we value as tourists. It encourages us to think critically about our impact on local communities and environments, and to seek out experiences that are authentic and meaningful.\",\n  \"questions\": [\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 36,\n      \"question\": \"What is the main idea of the article?\",\n      \"options\": [\"Slow travel is a way to see as many places as possible in a short amount of time.\", \"Slow travel involves taking the time to really experience a place and its culture.\", \"Slow travel is only for wealthy people who can afford to take their time.\", \"The article does not mention slow travel at all.\"],\n      \"answer\": \"option2\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 37,\n      \"question\": \"What might a slow traveler do instead of visiting a famous museum or landmark?\",\n      \"options\": [\"They might go shopping for souvenirs.\", \"They might take a cooking class with a local family.\", \"They might attend a traditional festival.\", \"They might visit another country.\"],\n      \"answer\": \"option2\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 38,\n      \"question\": \"What is one way that slow travelers can reduce their impact on the environment?\",\n      \"options\": [\"By staying in luxury hotels and using private cars.\", \"By choosing eco-friendly accommodations and transportation options.\", \"By eating at fast food restaurants.\", \"By not visiting any tourist destinations.\"],\n      \"answer\": \"option2\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 39,\n      \"question\": \"What is a potential criticism of slow travel?\",\n      \"options\": [\"It's too expensive for most people.\", \"It's only suitable for young people.\", \"It's not environmentally friendly.\", \"It's not a good way to see many places.\"],\n      \"answer\": \"option1\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 40,\n      \"question\": \"What does the article suggest is a way to experience slow travel on a budget?\",\n      \"options\": [\"Staying in luxury hotels and using private cars.\", \"Participating in community-based tourism initiatives or staying in hostels.\", \"Visiting expensive tourist destinations during peak season.\", \"Not traveling at all.\"],\n      \"answer\": \"option2\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
